notes.body:
As long as the patch will:

- treat all packets with the `CANXL_XLF` (0x80) bit set in the 5th octet of the SocketCAN header as being CAN XL frames;
- treat all non-CAN XL frames with the `CANFD_FDF` (0x04) bit set in the 6th octet of the SocketCAN header as being CAN FD frames (with the possible exception of frames where that octet can be believed to be uninitialized garbage - I have to check whether older versions of the kernel would provide uninitialized garbage in that octet);
- tread all non-CAN XL frames with that bit not set, but with an "original length" (rather than "captured length") of 72 (8+64) octets, as CAN FD frames;
- tread all other frames as CAN CC;

*without* requiring all CAN FD frames to have an "original length" of 72 (8+64) octets and requiring all CAN CC frames to have an "original length" of 16 (8+8) octets, that will be sufficient to allow non-pcap/pcapng capture file readers of files containing CAN packets to prepend a SocketCAN header to the frame data, return the resulting packet as a `WTAP_ENCAP_SOCKETCAN` packet, and have it properly dissected, that's sufficient.

(Note that this is not working around an problem with the BLF file format, it's working around the only way to provide CAN frames from libwireshark *currently* being to provide them as `WTAP_ENCAP_SOCKETCAN` frames.)

> treat all packets with the CANXL_XLF (0x80) bit set in the 5th octet of the SocketCAN header as being CAN XL frames

Yes. When the length is also within the range of possible valid values (12 + 1 to 12 + 2048)

> treat all non-CAN XL frames with the `CANFD_FDF` (0x04) bit set in the 6th octet of the SocketCAN header as being CAN FD frames

Yes. When the length is also within the range of possible valid values (8 + 0 to 8 + 64)

> (with the possible exception of frames where that octet can be believed to be uninitialized garbage - I have to check whether older versions of the kernel would provide uninitialized garbage in that octet)

There is no uninitialized garbage introduced by the Linux kernel. But you can always forge arbitrary data with the use of PF_PACKET sockets. Therefore the only safe way is to properly set or clear the CANFD_FDF flag in libpcap depending on the LINUX_SLL_P_CAN and LINUX_SLL_P_CANFD types.

This is the reason why I was always pushing to use the frame size (16/72) for detecting CAN CC and CAN FD. But with the option to allow non-padded CAN frames this approach has been dropped in DLT_CAN_SOCKETCAN. Now we have to live with this uncertainty. But this should not be a "real world" problem at all.

> tread all non-CAN XL frames with that bit not set, but with an "original length" (rather than "captured length") of 72 (8+64) octets, as CAN FD frames;

Yes. This is done in that way.

> tread all other frames as CAN CC

Yes. When the length is also within the range of possible valid values (8 + 0 to 8 + 8)

> without requiring all CAN FD frames to have an "original length" of 72 (8+64) octets and requiring all CAN CC frames to have an "original length" of 16 (8+8) octets,

Yes, this is allowed through the above described length validations now.

> that will be sufficient to allow non-pcap/pcapng capture file readers of files containing CAN packets to prepend a SocketCAN header to the frame data, return the resulting packet as a WTAP_ENCAP_SOCKETCAN packet, and have it properly dissected, that's sufficient.

Yes. Below is the relevant code which does the checks as described above. This snippet is easy to understand and to review.

It checks for CANXL_XLF and CANFD_FDF and also the frame length of 72 (when CANFD_FDF is not set).
And in the end the frames also also checked by valid sizes which either works with padded or non-padded content.

```
        if (canxl_flags & CANXL_XLF) {
            /* CAN XL: check for min/max data length */
            if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 13) && (tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 2060))
                can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN_XL;
        } else {
            /* CAN CC/FD */
            if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) == 72) || (canfd_flags & CANFD_FDF)) {
                /* CAN FD: check for min/max data length */
                if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 8) && (tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 72))
                    can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN_FD;
            } else if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 8) && (tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 16))
                can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN;
        }
```

I think this is what you requested.

> There is no uninitialized garbage introduced by the Linux kernel.

So, in all receive code paths in all versions of the Linux kernel, starting with 2.6.27, all the `struct can_frame` bytes between the `can_dlc` field and the `data` field in an skbuff for a packet will have been zeroed out before the skbuff is handed to the socket's receive routine?  In 2.6.27 `struct can_frame` is

```
/**
 * struct can_frame - basic CAN frame structure
 * @can_id:  the CAN ID of the frame and CAN_*_FLAG flags, see above.
 * @can_dlc: the data length field of the CAN frame
 * @data:    the CAN frame payload.
 */
struct can_frame {
	canid_t can_id;  /* 32 bit CAN_ID + EFF/RTR/ERR flags */
	__u8    can_dlc; /* data length code: 0 .. 8 */
	__u8    data[8] __attribute__((aligned(8)));
};
```

so those bytes aren't part of any named field - they're just 3 bytes of padding inserted to align the `data` field on an 8-byte boundary.

